From 134a4b503a7bb86b3cc3a7d1713127a5971792ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:07:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] linuxkpi: dma_map_sg cannot map multi-page lists on
 non-coherent arm64

Two independent defects on the same path, both hardware-verified on a Banana Pi
M64 (Allwinner A64) driving a Mali-400 through drm-kmod and Mesa/lima.

1. nsegments = 1 makes a multi-page dma_map_sg() IMPOSSIBLE.

The coupling is not visible from linux_dma_tag_init(). busdma bounds the per-map
SYNC LIST by dmat->common.nsegments, and sync_count ACCUMULATES across calls on
the same map:

    } else if ((map->flags & DMAMAP_COHERENT) == 0) {
            if (map->sync_count == 0 || curaddr != sl_end) {
                    if (++map->sync_count > dmat->common.nsegments)
                            break;      -> buflen != 0 -> EFBIG

linux_dma_map_sg_attrs() loads every S/G entry into one shared map, so entry 0
takes the single sync-list slot and entry 1 fails. Measured, with a diagnostic
added to the failure path:

    lkpi dma_map_sg: load_phys failed err=27 i=1/64 phys=0x54c02000 len=4096

Failure on the SECOND entry of a 64-entry list, every run. A coherent map never
touches that counter, which is why this only bites non-coherent devices -- i.e.
essentially every arm64 SoC GPU. Raised to 1024; nsegments also sizes the map
allocation (sizeof(*map) + sizeof(struct sync_list) * nsegments), which is
affordable because the sync list only grows on a DISCONTIGUITY.

2. The failure paths leave sgl->dma_map stale, and unmap validates nothing.

linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs() goes straight to bus_dmamap_sync(priv->dmat,
sgl->dma_map, ...). The map function returns 0 on failure leaving that field bad
three ways: bounce_bus_dmamap_create() returns ENOMEM from its dmat->segments
allocation WITHOUT writing *mapp at all; alloc_dmamap() failure leaves NULL; and
the load_phys path destroys the map but leaves the pointer reachable. A caller
whose teardown then unmaps -- which drm/lima does -- dereferences it:

    far 0x1  esr 0x96000004      elr bounce_bus_dmamap_sync+0x34c
                                 lr  linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs+0xac

i.e. the field held the literal value 1. Now cleared on every failure path, with
a NULL guard in unmap and a clear after a successful unmap so a double unmap is a
no-op rather than a use-after-free.

Effect on the board: six consecutive runs with no panic where the same sequence
previously killed it, and with the segment fix eglCreateContext succeeds --
GL_RENDERER = Mali400, OpenGL ES 2.0 Mesa 26.2.0.

Also keeps a temporary printf on both failure paths. The caller only ever sees
Linux's generic 0, which is what made the EGL_BAD_ALLOC unexplainable from
userspace; worth removing once this is settled.
---
 sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c | 99 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 94 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
index c4f0564c7..fc8666c76 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
+++ b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
@@ -211,6 +211,44 @@ linux_pdev_dma_init(struct pci_dev *pdev)
 	return (error);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Segment budget for the tag every LinuxKPI DMA mapping goes through.
+ *
+ * This was 1, and on a NON-COHERENT arm64 map that makes a multi-page
+ * dma_map_sg() IMPOSSIBLE -- not slow, not degraded, impossible. The coupling is
+ * not obvious from here: busdma's bounce backend bounds the per-map SYNC LIST by
+ * dmat->common.nsegments,
+ *
+ *     } else if ((map->flags & DMAMAP_COHERENT) == 0) {
+ *             if (map->sync_count == 0 || curaddr != sl_end) {
+ *                     if (++map->sync_count > dmat->common.nsegments)
+ *                             break;      -> buflen != 0 -> EFBIG
+ *
+ * and sync_count ACCUMULATES across calls on the same map. linux_dma_map_sg_attrs()
+ * deliberately loads every S/G entry into one shared map, so entry 0 takes the
+ * single sync-list slot and entry 1 fails with EFBIG. The caller only ever sees
+ * dma_map_sg() returning 0.
+ *
+ * Measured on a Mali-400 (lima) via drm-kmod, aarch64:
+ *   lkpi dma_map_sg: load_phys failed err=27 i=1/64 phys=0x54c02000 len=4096
+ * i.e. failure on the SECOND entry of a 64-entry list, every single time. It
+ * surfaced in userspace as EGL_BAD_ALLOC from eglCreateContext with no
+ * explanation, and (before a separate fix) as a kernel panic when the caller's
+ * teardown then unmapped the map that was never created.
+ *
+ * nsegments also sizes the map allocation --
+ * sizeof(*map) + sizeof(struct sync_list) * nsegments -- so this is a real cost
+ * per map, not a free constant. It is affordable because the sync list only grows
+ * on a DISCONTIGUITY: physically adjacent pages extend the current entry rather
+ * than adding one. 1024 entries is 32 KiB per map and covers a fully fragmented
+ * 4 MiB mapping, far past anything this path sees, while coalescing makes the
+ * effective reach much larger.
+ *
+ * A coherent map never touches this counter, which is why the defect only shows
+ * up on non-coherent devices -- i.e. essentially every arm64 SoC GPU.
+ */
+#define	LINUX_DMA_TAG_NSEGMENTS	1024
+
 int
 linux_dma_tag_init(struct device *dev, u64 dma_mask)
 {
@@ -234,7 +272,7 @@ linux_dma_tag_init(struct device *dev, u64 dma_mask)
 	    BUS_SPACE_MAXADDR,		/* highaddr */
 	    NULL, NULL,			/* filtfunc, filtfuncarg */
 	    BUS_SPACE_MAXSIZE,		/* maxsize */
-	    1,				/* nsegments */
+	    LINUX_DMA_TAG_NSEGMENTS,	/* nsegments -- NOT 1, see the #define */
 	    BUS_SPACE_MAXSIZE,		/* maxsegsz */
 	    0,				/* flags */
 	    NULL, NULL,			/* lockfunc, lockfuncarg */
@@ -1902,6 +1940,7 @@ linux_dma_map_sg_attrs(struct device *dev, struct scatterlist *sgl, int nents,
 	struct linux_dma_priv *priv;
 	struct scatterlist *sg;
 	int i, nseg;
+	int lkpi_dmasg_err;	/* BZDOS DIAGNOSTIC (temporary) */
 	bus_dma_segment_t seg;
 
 	priv = dev->dma_priv;
@@ -1909,7 +1948,26 @@ linux_dma_map_sg_attrs(struct device *dev, struct scatterlist *sgl, int nents,
 	DMA_PRIV_LOCK(priv);
 
 	/* create common DMA map in the first S/G entry */
-	if (bus_dmamap_create(priv->dmat, 0, &sgl->dma_map) != 0) {
+	/* BZDOS DIAGNOSTIC (temporary): which failure path actually fires, with the
+	 * real errno, rate-limited so a hot caller cannot flood the console. The
+	 * caller only ever sees "0", i.e. Linux's generic failure, which is what made
+	 * the Mali/lima EGL_BAD_ALLOC unexplainable from userspace. */
+	if ((lkpi_dmasg_err = bus_dmamap_create(priv->dmat, 0, &sgl->dma_map)) != 0) {
+		static int warned;
+		if (warned++ < 8)
+			printf("lkpi dma_map_sg: bus_dmamap_create failed err=%d "
+			    "nents=%d dir=%d\n", lkpi_dmasg_err, nents, direction);
+		/*
+		 * Do NOT leave a stale pointer behind. bus_dmamap_create()'s
+		 * bounce implementation has a failure path that returns ENOMEM
+		 * WITHOUT writing *mapp at all (the dmat->segments allocation),
+		 * so on that path sgl->dma_map keeps whatever the caller's memory
+		 * happened to hold -- including a map this function destroyed on
+		 * an earlier attempt. linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs() validates
+		 * nothing, so a caller that unmaps after a failed map (a normal
+		 * teardown shape) dereferences that stale value and panics.
+		 */
+		sgl->dma_map = NULL;
 		DMA_PRIV_UNLOCK(priv);
 		return (0);
 	}
@@ -1917,11 +1975,18 @@ linux_dma_map_sg_attrs(struct device *dev, struct scatterlist *sgl, int nents,
 	/* load all S/G list entries */
 	for_each_sg(sgl, sg, nents, i) {
 		nseg = -1;
-		if (_bus_dmamap_load_phys(priv->dmat, sgl->dma_map,
-		    sg_phys(sg), sg->length, BUS_DMA_NOWAIT,
-		    &seg, &nseg) != 0) {
+		if ((lkpi_dmasg_err = _bus_dmamap_load_phys(priv->dmat,
+		    sgl->dma_map, sg_phys(sg), sg->length, BUS_DMA_NOWAIT,
+		    &seg, &nseg)) != 0) {
+			static int warned2;
+			if (warned2++ < 8)
+				printf("lkpi dma_map_sg: load_phys failed err=%d "
+				    "i=%d/%d phys=%#lx len=%u nseg=%d\n",
+				    lkpi_dmasg_err, i, nents,
+				    (unsigned long)sg_phys(sg), sg->length, nseg);
 			bus_dmamap_unload(priv->dmat, sgl->dma_map);
 			bus_dmamap_destroy(priv->dmat, sgl->dma_map);
+			sgl->dma_map = NULL;	/* destroyed: must not stay reachable */
 			DMA_PRIV_UNLOCK(priv);
 			return (0);
 		}
@@ -1963,6 +2028,29 @@ linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs(struct device *dev, struct scatterlist *sgl,
 
 	priv = dev->dma_priv;
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing here validated sgl->dma_map before using it. linux_dma_map_
+	 * sg_attrs() returns 0 on failure (Linux semantics), and a caller that
+	 * then runs its teardown path -- which drm/lima does -- arrives here with
+	 * a map that was never created, or was created and then destroyed on an
+	 * error path. The result is a dereference of a stale pointer inside
+	 * bus_dmamap_sync().
+	 *
+	 * Observed on a Mali-400 (lima) context creation, aarch64:
+	 *   far 0x1  esr 0x96000004   (read, translation fault level 0)
+	 *   elr bounce_bus_dmamap_sync+0x34c
+	 *   lr  linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs+0xac
+	 * i.e. sgl->dma_map held the literal value 1. The same underlying map
+	 * failure otherwise surfaces harmlessly as EGL_BAD_ALLOC; whether it
+	 * panics depends only on whether the caller's cleanup reaches this
+	 * function.
+	 *
+	 * Unmapping something that was never mapped is the caller's bug, but the
+	 * kernel must not turn it into a panic when the check costs one branch.
+	 */
+	if (sgl->dma_map == NULL)
+		return;
+
 	DMA_PRIV_LOCK(priv);
 
 	if ((attrs & DMA_ATTR_SKIP_CPU_SYNC) != 0)
@@ -1986,6 +2074,7 @@ linux_dma_unmap_sg_attrs(struct device *dev, struct scatterlist *sgl,
 
 	bus_dmamap_unload(priv->dmat, sgl->dma_map);
 	bus_dmamap_destroy(priv->dmat, sgl->dma_map);
+	sgl->dma_map = NULL;	/* makes a double unmap a no-op, not a UAF */
 	DMA_PRIV_UNLOCK(priv);
 }
 
-- 
2.55.0

